              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sale price</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$699 at the top of the page; $599 in the page’s comparison table</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflict on Horizon’s page</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The agreement on the hardware is strong, and the 2025 review’s dimensions match Horizon’s
            current table to the half-inch. The conflicts are about software and price, not about the
            machine you would be standing on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-deck-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The 55-Inch Deck Decides Who It Suits</h2>
<p>
            Fifty-five inches is a common belt length at this price and it is enough for walking. It
            becomes a constraint as your stride lengthens, and for most adults it becomes a problem
            somewhere around a steady run.
          </p>
          <p>
            The arithmetic is straightforward. An adult of around 5 ft 10 in. covers roughly three feet
            per stride at a walk, closer to four feet at an easy jog, and nearer five feet at a genuine
            run. On a 55-inch belt the walking case has comfortable margin, the jogging case has some,
            and the running case has very little — which is when people start shortening their stride
            to stay clear of the rear roller without noticing they are doing it. That shortened gait is
            where treadmill running earns its reputation for feeling unnatural.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both independent reviews we read describe the T101 as suited to walking and light jogging
            rather than regular running, and the 10 mph ceiling points the same way. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            works through the stride arithmetic, and the short version is that anyone over about
            5 ft 8 in. who intends to run regularly should be looking at a 60-inch deck. Horizon’s own
            T202 and 7.0 AT both have one, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running guide</a>
            explains why deck length outranks almost every other figure for that use.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this is a criticism of the T101. It is a criticism of buying it for the wrong job.
            For walking, incline walking and a mile or two of easy jogging, the deck is not the
            limiting factor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Console, Apps and the No-Subscription Point</h2>
<p>
            The T101 Connect has no subscription. The console is four LED windows showing speed,
            incline, time, distance, calories and heart rate, with six built-in programs, Bluetooth
            speakers, a USB charging port and a tablet holder, and it does the same job in year five as
            on day one with no account attached. That is worth real money and it is rarely priced into
            a comparison: a cheaper machine with a membership behind its screen can cost more over five
            years. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to subscription-free treadmills</a>
            sets out that arithmetic.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What Bluetooth does and does not do</h3>
          <p>
            The Connect sends live speed, incline and distance over Bluetooth FTMS, the open fitness
            machine standard, and Horizon lists JRNY, Peloton, Zwift and Kinomap as compatible apps,
            plus syncing with Strava. It is the only current Horizon treadmill that lists JRNY, the
            adaptive-workout app owned by Horizon’s parent company; JRNY and the other apps are
            separate, optional services with their own prices. Horizon lists no Apple Watch or Samsung
            Galaxy Watch pairing for the T101 Connect.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two limitations are worth knowing before you buy for an app. First, receiving data is not
            the same as being controlled by an app: the independent review of the current model found
            that JRNY could start and stop the belt but not set speed or incline, and that only Kinomap
            changed the incline. Second, the T101’s owner’s manual explains that its Bluetooth
            heart-rate mode and its tablet mode are switched with the Bluetooth button, and the same
            review reports that a heart-rate strap pairs only when no app is connected. If you want a
            strap and an app at once, expect to route the strap through the app instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trade-off of a plain console is that you supply the motivation. There is no trainer on
            a screen. If guided sessions are what gets you on the machine, price that membership into
            the decision, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">workout library</a>
            is a free starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What It Is Built to Do: Walking and Incline Walking</h2>
<p>
            Walking is not a low-demand use. An hour at 3 mph on a 10% incline is a long, steady load
            on a drive system, because incline shifts work onto the motor and duration is what builds
            heat. That is exactly the use the T101 is sold for, and the lifetime motor warranty is
            Horizon’s commitment that the motor will cope with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Horizon describes the deck as three-zone Variable Response Cushioning: softer where the foot
            lands and firmer where it pushes off. That is the right design intent for walking, where you
            want some give without the deck feeling like it swallows the step. The 2024 review we read
            found the cushioning comfortable; we have not stood on it ourselves.
          </p>
          <p>
            Ten percent of incline is enough for serious incline walking, which for most people is the
            most productive thing a treadmill offers in the time they have. The popular
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            calls for 12%, which the T101 cannot reach, so if that routine is the plan, look at the T202
            (12%) or a 15% machine. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill roundup</a>
            compares the T101 with machines built for the same job.
          </p>
          <p>
            Noise is one area where we cannot add a first-hand view. The owner’s manual sets the
            machine’s unloaded noise at no more than 70 dB at 7.5 mph, and one reviewer measured 57 dB
            at 3 mph rising to 70 dB at 10 mph without a user on the belt. On a shared floor, footfall
            travelling into the structure usually matters more than motor noise;
            <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our quiet treadmill guide</a>
            covers what helps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folding-and-space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Folding, Footprint and Setting It Up</h2>
<p>
            Using Horizon’s figures, the T101 Connect takes up about 16.4 square feet of floor in use
            (70.5 by 33.5 inches) and about 10.9 square feet folded (46 by 34 inches). It folds with
            hydraulic assistance and stands 61 inches tall when stowed, which rules out most under-stair
            plans. At 180 lb it is light for a full-size treadmill but still heavy; the transport wheels
            work on hard floors and much less well on carpet.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clearance the manual asks for</h3>
          <p>
            The T101 owner’s manual asks for a clear zone behind the treadmill at least as wide as the
            machine and at least 79 inches (2 metres) long, about two feet of access on each side, and
            one foot in front for the power cord. It also says the treadmill should not be placed in a
            garage, covered patio or anywhere outdoors. For ceilings, Horizon’s help centre advises
            adding 12 inches to the height of the tallest user; with a 7 1/8-inch step-on height and
            10% of incline, more margin is sensible in a basement with beams or ducts.
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Our dimensions guide</a>
            covers the general figures.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon specifies a 120 V, 60 Hz supply and says the T101 is not compatible with GFCI
            outlets. The manual asks for a dedicated circuit of at least 15 amps and no extension cord,
            adapter or surge strip. Garages, basements and bathrooms often have GFCI outlets, so check
            before delivery day. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">extension cord and circuit guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery and assembly</h3>
          <p>
            The box is 73 by 30 by 12 inches and weighs about 220 lb. Horizon offers standard delivery to
            the door, room-of-choice delivery, and white-glove delivery with assembly, priced at
            checkout. It says assembly takes 30 minutes or less, and the reviews we read describe it as
            easy, though a second person helps. Read the manual’s warning before you start: the frame
            is shipped with red transport cables that stop the high-pressure fold shocks springing the
            base open, and they stay on until the step that tells you to remove them. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembling a treadmill</a>
            covers the general job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The October 2022 Recall of the T101-05</h2>
<p>
            On 27 October 2022 the US Consumer Product Safety Commission and Johnson Health Tech
            Trading, Horizon’s importer, announced a recall of Horizon T101-05 folding treadmills
            (CPSC recall number 23-026). This section sets out what the notice says. It concerns the
            T101-05 only; the current T101 Connect is a later version and is not named in it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What was recalled, and why</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Hazard.</strong> The treadmills can unexpectedly accelerate, change speed or stop without user input, posing a fall hazard.</li>
            <li><strong>Units.</strong> About 192,000 in the US, plus about 7,000 in Canada, sold from March 2018 through October 2022 for between about $600 and $1,000 at Dick’s Sporting Goods, Johnson Fitness &amp; Wellness, horizonfitness.com and Amazon.</li>
            <li><strong>Serial numbers.</strong> Only T101-05 units whose serial numbers begin <strong>TM734</strong> (made in China) or <strong>TM486</strong> (made in Vietnam) are included.</li>
            <li><strong>Reports.</strong> Horizon had received at least 874 reports of the treadmills changing speed or stopping unexpectedly, including 71 reports of injury, among them bruises, abrasions and two broken bones.</li>
            <li><strong>Remedy.</strong> A repair: stop using the treadmill and contact Horizon for a free USB drive with a software repair to install. It is not a refund or a replacement.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to check your serial number</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Unplug the treadmill. The model name and serial number are on a label on the metal frame near the power switch, at the front of the machine.</li>
            <li>Check that the label says <strong>T101-05</strong>. Other T101 versions are not part of this recall.</li>
            <li>Read the first five characters of the serial number. If they are TM734 or TM486, the unit is in the recall unless the software repair has already been installed.</li>
            <li>Contact Horizon. The recall notice gives horizonfitness.com/support/recall (which on 14 October 2026 redirected to Horizon’s T101-05 software-update page) and Horizon’s recall line on 888-223-1045, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Central, Monday to Friday. Horizon’s product pages also link a "Safety Recalls" notice in the footer.</li>
          </ol>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the update involves</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s support pages list two T101-05 software builds, S2.09 and S9.02, and say which
            applies depends on whether the label reads TM734 or TM486. Horizon’s printed instructions for
            S2.09 use a FAT32-formatted USB stick inserted into the port on the right of the console with
            the treadmill on; the console shows "U-d15", you press Enter, and after a reboot it shows the
            new software version. If the console will not update, Horizon offers to post a USB stick with
            the software already loaded. Horizon’s general software page warns that updates reset the
            console to factory settings and erase saved user data. If you are not comfortable doing it,
            ask Horizon for the pre-loaded stick and follow its instructions exactly.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Buying a used T101</h3>
          <p>
            The practical risk now is a second-hand T101-05 whose owner never installed the repair. Ask
            for a photograph of the serial label before you travel, check the prefix, and ask whether the
            update was done and when. If the seller does not know, assume it was not, and contact
            Horizon before you use the machine. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers the rest of the inspection.
          </p>
          <p>
            One further fact is on the public record. On 4 August 2026 the CPSC announced that Johnson
            Health Tech Trading had agreed to a $16.875 million civil penalty, provisionally accepted,
            resolving charges that it failed to report the T101-05 hazard to the agency immediately. That
            is about how quickly the company told the regulator, not about the repair itself; our
            <a href="/is-horizon-a-good-treadmill-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon brand assessment</a>
            sets out what it means for the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="manual" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Horizon T101 Treadmill Manual: Where to Get It and What It Covers</h2>
<p>
            Horizon publishes the T101 manuals itself, and the right one depends on your version. Start
            by reading the model name on the serial label near the power switch (T101-05, T101-06,
            T101-07, T101-08 and so on), then use that exact name when you search.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where to find it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Horizon’s help centre</strong> at help.horizonfitness.com, reached from the "Support Home" link in the footer of horizonfitness.com, has a page for each T101 version with the owner’s manual (or "owner’s guide") and a shorter "treadmill guide" as PDFs, plus exploded parts diagrams for some versions. On 14 October 2026 it listed pages for the T101-05 (2018), T101-06, T101-07 and T101-08 Connect, and a T101 Connect general-information page.</li>
            <li><strong>The "Product Manuals" link</strong> in Horizon’s site footer goes to parts.horizonfitness.com, Horizon’s replacement-parts site, where you choose the brand and machine type to find parts for your model.</li>
            <li><strong>The box.</strong> The manual ships with the machine. If you are buying used, ask for it, but download the PDF for your version anyway, because the documents have been revised.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Third-party manual sites also carry copies. Use Horizon’s own file where you can, since a
            mirrored PDF may be for a different version. If a help-centre page will not load from a
            search result, reach it from the "Support Home" link in the footer of horizonfitness.com.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the T101 manual covers</h3>
          <p>
            The T101-08 owner’s manual, which we read in full, runs to about 45 pages in English and
            covers: safety precautions and the in-home-use-only condition; electrical requirements;
            unpacking and a seven-step assembly with the hardware list; placement and clearances;
            folding, unfolding and moving; using the safety key; levelling; console operation and the
            program profiles; Bluetooth heart-rate, speaker and app connection; the maintenance
            schedule, lubrication, belt tensioning and centring; troubleshooting; and the warranty. The
            shorter T101-05 treadmill guide covers safety, lubrication and troubleshooting.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Key settings and procedures</h3>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Task</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What Horizon’s documents say</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The treadmill will not start without it. Clip it to your clothing; it cuts power if you fall. Test it every two weeks and remove it when the machine is not in use.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Starting</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stand on the side rails, insert the key, press Start for a quick start, or choose a program and press Enter.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Resetting the console</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hold Stop for 3 seconds.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubrication</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T101-08 manual: every 3 months or 150 miles, with 100% silicone, half a bottle per application, zig-zagged under the belt. The 2018 T101-05 guide says every 3 months or 300 miles. Follow the document for your version.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">"LUBE BELT" message</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The treadmill will not run while it shows. After lubricating, hold Speed Up and Stop together for about 5-7 seconds to clear it.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt tension</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">With the belt stopped, turn both rear roller bolts a quarter turn clockwise, then test; never more than a quarter turn at a time, and never over-tighten.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt centring</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">At no more than 1 mph, turn the left bolt a quarter turn (anticlockwise if the belt has drifted right, clockwise if left) and wait for it to settle.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Miles or kilometres</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon’s help centre: hold Incline Up and Speed Down for 3-5 seconds, go to ENG3, choose P1, select units, and hold Stop for 3 seconds to save.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Set incline to zero first; lift the rear until the latch locks. Move it only folded and latched.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Error messages and codes</h3>
          <p>
            The T101 manuals are organised by symptom rather than by a numbered error list. The messages
            they explain are "SAFETY KEY OFF" or dashes on the display (the key is missing or not seated),
            "LUBE" or "LUBE BELT" (the lubrication reminder), and, in the T101-05 guide, "Speed Range
            Error – Ending Workout", a safety cut-out when load and steep incline push the belt speed out
            of range; the fix is to lower the incline. Horizon’s help centre adds stuck-key error codes
            for the T101-07 from software S4.09, which point to a faulty console overlay. For anything
            else — a belt that stops and starts, a blank console, a tripped breaker — the manual’s
            first checks are the same: a dedicated 15-amp circuit, no GFCI outlet, no power strip or
            extension cord, and secure console cable connections. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safety key guide</a>
            go further. Unplug the machine before removing any cover.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Warranty and Keeping It Running</h2>
<p>
            The warranty split tells you where to pay attention. Lifetime frame and motor cover reflects
            real confidence in those parts. One year of parts and labour is short, and it means that
            from year two the wear items are yours: the belt, the deck surface, the rollers and the
            console. Horizon’s help centre adds that the warranty covers home use only, and the manual
            says using the machine above its rated capacity, outdoors or in a commercial setting voids
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every one of those wear items is maintenance-sensitive. A deck run without lubricant loads
            the motor and wears the belt from underneath, which is the commonest way a machine in this
            class dies early. Follow the interval in your version’s manual —
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the walkthrough</a>
            covers the method. Belt slipping and belt tracking are different faults with different
            fixes; work through
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">slipping versus tracking</a>
            rather than tightening until something stops.
          </p>
          <p>
            The manual’s monthly checklist includes unplugging, waiting 60 seconds and vacuuming dust
            from the motor compartment, which it says prevents premature failure of electrical parts.
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">The full owner’s schedule</a>
            takes about ten minutes a month. Register the machine with Horizon too, so you are contacted
            directly if a future safety notice affects it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy It, and Who Should Not</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are walking.</strong> Daily walking, incline walking and the odd easy
            jog in a spare room, on a machine that asks nothing of you afterwards, is what the T101 is
            for — if you can find it in stock.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it to run on.</strong> The 55-inch deck and 10 mph ceiling are the design
            envelope. Runners should look at Horizon’s T202 or 7.0 AT, both with 60-inch decks, in our
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon range comparison</a>,
            or at the
            <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6</a>
            from the same parent company.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy it if guided content is what gets you moving.</strong> The console is
            deliberately plain. Apps receive its data but, in the reviewer’s testing, mostly cannot
            control it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>While it is sold out,</strong> do not pay a reseller premium for it. Cross-shop the
            walking-first field in
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the walking roundup</a>
            and <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our under-$1,000 guide</a>,
            and if your budget is lower, read
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad or treadmill</a>
            first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Horizon T101 still available?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon still lists it, as the T101 Connect, but on 14 October 2026 its US store showed it sold out at $699 against a $999 reference price, with no restock date. Dick’s Sporting Goods carries a listing; we could not confirm stock there. Check the serial number on any older or used unit against the 2022 T101-05 recall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I find the Horizon T101 treadmill manual?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On Horizon’s help centre, help.horizonfitness.com, which has a page for each T101 version (T101-05, -06, -07 and -08 Connect) with the owner’s manual as a PDF. Find your version on the serial label near the power switch first, because lubrication intervals and software differ between versions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does the Horizon T101 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon no longer publishes a motor rating for the T101 Connect; it describes a Johnson Drive System and covers the motor with a lifetime warranty. One independent reviewer reports a 2.5 HP figure on the motor label. We treat that as unconfirmed and judge the machine on deck, capacity and warranty instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is my Horizon T101 part of the recall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the label on the frame near the power switch says T101-05 and the serial number starts with TM734 or TM486. Those units could change speed or stop unexpectedly. The remedy is a free software repair from Horizon, by download or a mailed USB stick; call 888-223-1045 or start from horizonfitness.com/support/recall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I lubricate a Horizon T101?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The T101-08 owner’s manual says every three months or 150 miles, using 100% silicone; the 2018 T101-05 guide says every three months or 300 miles. The console shows "LUBE BELT" when it is due and will not run until you clear it by holding Speed Up and Stop for about five to seven seconds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Horizon T101 good for running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for regular running. The 20 by 55-inch deck and 10 mph ceiling suit walking, incline walking and light jogging, and both independent reviews we read say the same. Anyone over about 5 ft 8 in. who plans to run should look for a 60-inch deck, such as Horizon’s T202 or 7.0 AT.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a Horizon T101</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from treating every T101 as the same machine, or from buying it for a job it was not designed for.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Downloading the wrong manual</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">T101-05, -06, -07 and -08 manuals differ, including on the lubrication interval and the software. Read the model name on the serial label first, then download that exact document from Horizon’s help centre rather than the first PDF a search turns up.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used T101-05 without checking the serial</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Units whose serial numbers begin TM734 or TM486 were recalled in 2022 for unexpected speed changes and stops. A seller may never have installed the free software repair. Ask for a photo of the label before you travel, and contact Horizon if you cannot confirm the update was done.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing it on a horsepower figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon no longer publishes one for the T101 Connect, so any number in a listing or review is not Horizon’s. Comparing it with another brand’s peak or test rating compares unlike things. The lifetime motor warranty is the more reliable signal.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging it into a GFCI outlet or power strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon says the T101 is not GFCI-compatible, and the manual asks for a dedicated 15-amp circuit with no extension cord or surge strip. Many "belt stops" and "won’t start" complaints trace back to the outlet, so fix that before calling for service.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

